AxiomOS: Layered Constitutional Enforcement
What the Kernel Can Enforce, What It Can't, and How AxiomOS Uses Both
Architecture Overview for Grant Reviewers and Technical Stakeholders
Justicar / AxiomOS Project — Phenarax-ui / QuietStatix


Executive Summary
AxiomOS is a constitutional AI operating system: a Linux-based OS in which governance rules are enforced by the operating system itself rather than left to individual applications. Most AI governance today lives inside one application and can be avoided by using a different one. AxiomOS moves enforcement down into the OS, where every application is subject to it.
This document explains how that enforcement works and is precise about its limits. The kernel is powerful but narrow: it can enforce exact rules about files, network access, and processes, and it cannot understand the meaning of what a program does. So AxiomOS uses four layers, each doing what it can actually guarantee:
Kernel-enforced limits — exact rules the kernel guarantees for every process
The constitutional gate — a protected system service that judges the meaning of actions against the constitution
Measured boot and remote attestation — so the network can tell whether a node is running the audited system
A signed, witnessed ledger — so history cannot be rewritten without other nodes noticing
Together these make the constitution very hard to get around, and make any attempt to get around it detectable. This document also says plainly what no operating system can guarantee, because a governance system that overstates its guarantees cannot be trusted.

The Core Problem: Rules That Live in One Application
Most AI governance systems are a piece of software running on top of an ordinary operating system. That creates real weaknesses:
The rules can be avoided by simply using different software
Records kept by an ordinary program can be edited by anything with enough permissions
Nothing stops other programs on the same machine from doing what the governed program is forbidden to do
AxiomOS addresses this by making enforcement part of the operating system, so it applies to every program on the node, not only the ones that opt in.

What the Kernel Can and Cannot See
Every program has to ask the kernel to do anything meaningful: open a file, send data over the network, start another program. That makes the kernel the one place every action passes through, and it is why kernel enforcement matters.
But the kernel sees these requests at a very low level. It sees "write these 2 MB to this file" or "send these bytes to this address." It does not see what the bytes mean. It cannot tell whether an image is a non-consensual deepfake (Article 7.1), whether a screen is manipulative (7.2), or whether data contains someone's location (7.5). That meaning only exists inside the application.
Speed matters too. A computer makes thousands to millions of these requests per second, and even a small AI model takes tens of milliseconds to judge one action. Running a model on every kernel request would make the system unusably slow.
So the honest design splits the work: the kernel enforces exact rules on every request, and a separate gate judges meaning for the smaller set of actions where meaning matters.

Layer 1: Kernel-Enforced Limits
These are guarantees in the strict sense. Linux already provides the mechanisms, and AxiomOS configures them from the constitution:
Mandatory access control (SELinux or AppArmor, plus Landlock for per-program limits) — which files and devices each program may touch. The gate, the ledger, and the constitution itself are protected so no ordinary program can modify them (Article 7.6).
System call filtering (seccomp) — which kinds of requests a program may make at all.
Network isolation (network namespaces and firewall rules) — which programs may reach the network, and where. Programs that are not explicitly permitted have no network access, which enforces local-first privacy (Article 2.3) by default.
Kernel encrypted tunnels (WireGuard, part of the Linux kernel) — founder and governance traffic is routed only through encrypted tunnels, and the firewall blocks it from leaving any other way (Article 8.2).
Sandboxing for AI agents and generated code — tools an agent uses run inside these limits, so even a fully misbehaving agent cannot reach outside what it was granted.
Where an action needs a judgment rather than a fixed rule, the kernel can pause the request and hand it to the gate (for example through seccomp user notification or fanotify permission events), and only continue once the gate answers.

Layer 2: The Constitutional Gate
The gate is a protected system service, not a part of the kernel. It evaluates the actions that matter — an agent running a command, a program sending data off the node, a request to generate media of a real person — against the constitution before they happen.
It works in two steps:
Deterministic rules in plain code hard-block known-dangerous actions and allow obviously safe ones. These are predictable and cannot be argued with.
A local AI model judges the cases that depend on meaning, such as whether a request would transmit personal data without consent.
It fails closed: if the gate cannot reach a decision, the action is denied. Irreversible actions require explicit yes/no confirmation from the person (Article 2.2).
What the gate does not claim: a model's judgment is a judgment, not a guarantee. It can be wrong, and text inside an action can try to manipulate it. AxiomOS limits that risk by keeping the deterministic rules first, running the gate itself inside Layer 1's protections, logging every decision, and requiring human confirmation where the stakes are highest. The rules about meaning are enforced carefully and auditably, not with certainty.

Layer 3: Measured Boot and Remote Attestation
The person who controls a machine can always change it. Someone with physical access or administrator rights can install a modified kernel, or run AxiomOS inside a virtual machine and alter it from outside. Article 10 also guarantees everyone the right to fork. No operating system can prevent this, and AxiomOS does not claim to.
What AxiomOS can do is make it visible to the network. Using the TPM security chip found in most modern computers, each node records exactly which software it booted (measured boot) and can prove that record to other nodes (remote attestation; Keylime is an existing open-source implementation). Combined with reproducible builds (Article 8.4), any node can check that a peer is running the audited, constitutionally sound release.
This turns "the rules can't be broken" into something that is actually true: a node that removes the constitutional layer can no longer prove it is running AxiomOS, and the network can decline to trust it with governance, contribution records, or private traffic.

Layer 4: A Signed, Witnessed Ledger
The Justicar ledger is append-only and hash-chained: each entry includes the hash of the one before it, so any edit, deletion, or reordering of past entries is detectable.
A hash chain alone shows tampering but does not prevent it — someone with full control of a machine could rewrite the entire chain and recompute every hash. AxiomOS closes that gap by having each node sign its entries and regularly publish its latest hash to other nodes. Once other nodes hold a signed copy of the chain's latest state, rewriting history requires fooling all of them. This is the same approach Certificate Transparency uses to keep web security certificates honest.
What this means in practice:
A committed entry cannot be silently changed — any change contradicts the copies other nodes hold
No administrator or founder can rewrite history without it being detected
Any node can audit the ledger, because its integrity can be checked rather than assumed

Contribution Tracking and the Axiom Coin Trust Cap
Contribution records, governance weight, and the trust cap are recorded through the gate and the ledger, on nodes that pass attestation. This prevents a modified node from injecting false records and makes every record auditable.
Two problems remain open, and this design does not claim to have solved them:
Proving each identity is a distinct person. A hardware key proves a device, not a human. Someone with money could buy many devices and hold many identities, each under the trust cap. Preventing this without collecting identity information is an unsolved problem across the field, and AxiomOS's approach to it is still under research.
Telling genuine contributions from fake ones. The system can prove that a record came from an attested node, but not that a real person did meaningful work — a script can complete lessons too. Contribution measurement will need review by people, not only by the OS.

The Mastery Engine as a System Service
AxiomOS ships with an integrated mastery engine — a personalized learning system that builds individual learner profiles and generates adaptive curriculum — as a built-in system service rather than an optional download.
Learner profiles are protected by Layer 1: the profile store is readable only by the mastery engine and the learner, and the engine has no network access unless the learner explicitly grants it. That makes "the system that teaches you cannot surveil you" a kernel-enforced fact about this service, not a promise.
Practical implications:
Available on every AxiomOS node without installation
Cannot transmit learner data without explicit consent — its network access is blocked at the kernel unless granted
Forkable and replaceable by design — architecture over personality cult

Distributed Network Implications
Because every node can prove which software it runs, trust between nodes rests on verification rather than reputation:
A node running the audited AxiomOS release can prove it, regardless of who operates it
A node that has removed or weakened the constitutional layer cannot prove it is running AxiomOS, and the network treats it accordingly
Ledger history is cross-checked between nodes, so no single node can rewrite it
Reproducible builds let anyone confirm that the audited source is what actually runs, directly addressing supply chain trust

What This Design Does Not Claim
To be explainable in plain language (Article 2.6), AxiomOS states its limits as clearly as its guarantees:
It cannot stop the owner of a machine from modifying that machine. It can make the modification detectable to the network.
It cannot guarantee AI judgments are correct. It runs them inside fixed limits, logs them, and asks people to confirm the irreversible ones.
It cannot yet prove that each participant is a distinct person. That remains open research.
It does not run AI models inside the kernel. The kernel enforces exact rules; the gate, a protected service, judges meaning.

Summary
AxiomOS moves governance out of individual applications and into the operating system, using each layer for what it can actually guarantee:
The kernel enforces exact limits on every program — files, network, processes
The constitutional gate judges the meaning of actions, fails closed, and asks people to confirm irreversible ones
Attestation lets the network detect any node that has removed the constitution
A signed, witnessed ledger makes rewriting history detectable
The result is a system where breaking the constitution is hard, and getting caught breaking it is the default. That is a stronger claim than application-layer governance can make, and every part of it can be built with technology that exists today.


